#!/usr/bin/python3
"""Set up this Mac's Touch ID calibration.

    aurora-touchid-setup                  set up what is missing
    aurora-touchid-setup --check          report only, write nothing
    aurora-touchid-setup --force-rebuild  rebuild and replace what differs
    add --json for a machine-readable result

The fingerprint sensor's driver loads per-device factory data that no
package can ship. The sensor node's firmware-name list in the device tree
names it, and the driver reads it as follows:
  - the first entry is the calibration (apple/mesa_calibration.bin when the
    node names none);
  - the second entry is the sensor's signed root key, which sensors with a
    nonce session load (MacBook Neo, M4 Pro). The driver's default is
    apple/msrk-j700.bin when the node names only the calibration.

Where the bytes come from, read-only:
  - Macs whose factory cache holds an FSC2 calibration (J700, J713, J616s).
    Each file is rebuilt from the factory records on the Hardware volume of
    the iBoot System Container, in the layout the enclave loads:
      comb { fdrd: IMG4 { IM4P, [0] seal-<CHIP>-<ECID> }, secb: trust bundle }
    The seal must name this Mac's CHIP, ECID and serial number and cover the
    record. The root key is rebuilt the same way from the MSRk record.
  - Other Macs (M1, M2) keep a comb record in the container, which is
    installed as carved.
The enclave alone verifies the signatures; these are provenance and
structure checks.

When it runs. It is never routine boot work:
  1. A file is missing. aurora-touchid-setup.service runs once at boot,
     before Touch ID starts. A boot where every file is in place skips it,
     on a condition that only checks that the files exist.
  2. The enclave refused the installed file. aurora-sep-ready finds the
     refusal in this boot's kernel log and starts the service once. The
     file is replaced, with a rollback copy kept. There is at most one
     automatic rebuild per boot.
  3. The owner runs it, with --check for a report or --force-rebuild to
     rebuild anyway.

A file this tool did not install is never replaced, unless the enclave
refused it this boot or --force-rebuild asks for it. Every replacement
keeps the old bytes in /var/lib/aurora-sep/rollback. The new file is
written atomically with mode 0600 and read back. The last result is in
/var/lib/aurora-sep/touchid-setup.json, with one JSON line per run in
touchid-setup.log.
"""
# The factory-record reader and the sealed-record rebuild below (the files,
# DER, APFS and admission sections) are Erik Bourget's work, first proposed
# in aurora-silicon/alarm-aurora#26, fedora-aurora#28 and nixos-aurora#27.
# This tool runs them on demand, adds the root key and keeps his checks.
